```c++
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

double findMedian(std::vector<double> &numArray, int size) { 
    // First we sort the array 
    std::sort(numArray.begin(), numArray.end()); 

    // Check if the number of elements is even or odd
    if (size % 2 != 0) 
        // If odd, the median is the middle number
        return (double)numArray[size/2]; 

    // If even, the median is the average of the two middle numbers
    return (double)(numArray[(size-1)/2] + numArray[size/2])/2.0; 
} 


// Usage
int main() {
    std::vector<double> vec = {1.5, 3.0, 4.5, 2.5, 5.0};
    double median = findMedian(vec, vec.size());
    std::cout << "Median: " << median << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function first sorts the input vector and then returns the median, which is either the middle value (for vectors of odd size) or the average of the two middle values (for vectors of even size). Note that sorting the input vector does not modify the original vector because a copy of the vector is passed to the function. If you do not want to alter the input vector but want to avoid copying, you can pass the vector by const reference and copy it inside the function.

