You can increment a number by 1 without using any arithmetic and bitwise operators by using the array indexing concept in C. The solution also involves pointers. Here's how you can do it:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int num = 0;
    // Declare an array and assign num to 0th index
    int arr[2] = {0};

    // Make a pointer point to the first index of the array
    int *ptr = arr;

    // Increment the pointer to reach next memory location
    ptr++;

    // Save the pointer value to num
    num = *ptr;

    printf("The incremented value is %d", num);
    return 0;
}
```
This will print "The incremented value is 1" because in C, pointers incremented to the next memory location point to the next index of an array. Since the array index starts from 0, when the pointer is incremented, it points to the 1st index of the array which has value 1. The value 1 is then saved in 'num'. If the pointer were initialized with 'arr' instead of 'arr + 1' resulting in the pointer pointing to the array index 0, the 'num' value would be 0. But with the pointer being incremented, it points to the next array index having value 1.

