Here is an example of a parametrized JUnit method. This test case uses JUnit 5 (JUnit Jupiter). We use `@ParameterizedTest` and `@MethodSource` annotations to provide input parameters for the test. 

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.MethodSource;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;

import java.util.stream.Stream;
import java.util.stream.Stream.of;

public class DivisionTest {

    private static final double DELTA = 0.0000001;

    private static Stream<Arguments> floatPairsProvider() {
        return Stream.of(
                Arguments.of(10.0f, 5.0f, 2.0),
                Arguments.of(15.0f, 2.0f, 7.5),
                Arguments.of(-10.0f, 2.0f, -5.0),
                Arguments.of(10.0f, 0.0f, Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY) // edge case
        );
    }

    @ParameterizedTest
    @MethodSource("floatPairsProvider")
    public void testDivision(float a, float b, double expected) {
        if (b == 0.0f) {
            ArithmeticException exception = assertThrows(ArithmeticException.class, () -> a / b);
            assertEquals("/ by zero", exception.getMessage());
        } else {
            double result = a / b;
            assertEquals(expected, result, DELTA);
        }
    }
}
```

A couple of things to note here:

1. `floatPairsProvider` returns a stream of Arguments, each argument comprising of the inputs (a and b) and the expected result (expected).
2. In the `testDivision` test, for the edge case when denominator is 0.0f, we assert that an ArithmeticException is thrown and its message is "/ by zero".
3. For non edge cases, we perform the division operation and then compare the result to the expected value, with a precision of up to seven decimal places. The `DELTA` constant is used to specify the precision.

