Here is a basic Less Mixin to create a gradient background and handle error cases:

```less
.create-gradient(@start: #000, @end: #fff, @direction: up) {
  @valid-directions: up, down, left, right;

  .direction() {
    @top:        bottom;
    @bottom:     top;
    @left:       right;
    @right:      left;
  }

  .error() {
    background: none;
    color: red;
    content: "Invalid direction parameter.";
  }

  when (@valid-directions = @direction),
  ((@direction = down) or (@direction = up)) 
  {
    .direction();
    background: linear-gradient(@direction[@direction], @start, @end);
  }
  when (@direction = left) or (@direction = right)
  {
    .direction();
    background: linear-gradient(to @direction[@direction], @start, @end);
  }
  default() {
    .error();
  }
}
```

In this example 
1) The create-gradient mixin takes three parameters, a starting color, an ending color, and the direction of the gradient. If no values are provided, the defaults will be used (Black to white from top to bottom).
2) The direction mixin is responsible for setting the gradient direction based on the input parameter. 
3) If 'up' or 'down' are used, the direction is set directly (Linear gradients default to 'to bottom'). If 'left' or 'right' are used, the 'to' prefix is added.
4) When invalid parameters are provided, the error mixin is used, which sets the background to none, the font color to red, and the content to a message reporting an invalid direction.

