Given a sorted and rotated array, it's best to use the modified version of Binary Search to find the element, because Binary Search has a time complexity of O(logN) which will be faster for large data sets compared to the linear search which has a time complexity of O(N). 

Here's a Java solution:

```java
public class Solution {
    public int[] searchRange(int[] nums, int target) {
        int leftIdx = binarySearch(nums, target, true);
        int rightIdx = binarySearch(nums, target, false) - 1;
        if (leftIdx <= rightIdx && rightIdx < nums.length && nums[leftIdx] == target && nums[rightIdx] == target) {
            return new int[]{leftIdx, rightIdx};
        }
        return new int[]{-1, -1};
    }

    public int binarySearch(int[] nums, int target, boolean left) {
        int lo = 0;
        int hi = nums.length;
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
            if (nums[mid] > target || (left && target == nums[mid])) {
                hi = mid;
            } else {
                lo = mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return lo;
    }
}
```

Here is the explanation of the code:

The `searchRange` function accepts a sorted and rotated array (`nums`) and the target number (`target`). It uses a `binarySearch` helper function to find the first (`leftIdx`) and last (`rightIdx`) index of the target number in this array.

If `nums[leftIdx]` and `nums[rightIdx]` are both equal to `target` and `rightIdx` is less than the length of `nums` array, then `{leftIdx, rightIdx}` array is returned. Otherwise, `{-1, -1}` is returned, indicating that the target is not found in `nums` array.

The `binarySearch` helper function accepts a sorted and rotated array (`nums`), the target number (`target`), and a boolean value (`left`), indicating whether we are searching for the first (`left = true`) or the last (`left = false`) index of `target`.

The function `binarySearch` returns the first index (`lo`) where `target` could be inserted in `nums` array without changing the current order of the array. Note that if `target` is found in the array, there could be multiple indices where it could be inserted without disrupting the current array order.

