&lt;p&gt;An optimal approximation to a real number $x$ for a specified denominator limit $d$ is represented as a rational number $\frac r s$ (in its simplest form) where $s \le d$. This implies that any other rational number $\frac p q$ that is nearer to $x$ than $\frac r s$ must have $q \gt d$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Typically, the optimal approximation to a real number is singularly determined for all denominator limits. However, there exist certain exceptions, for instance, $\frac 9 {40}$ has the dual optimal approximations $\frac 1 4$ and $\frac 1 5$ for the denominator limit $6$.
