For this problem, you will need to use the distance formula to calculate the distance between each consecutive pair of points and add those distances together to give the perimeter of the polygon. 

The distance between two points (x1, y1) and (x2, y2) is sqrt((x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2).

Here is a simple Java snippet that calculates the perimeter of an irregular polygon:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Initialise array of points.
        // You can replace these points with any points you have.
        int[][] points = {
                {4, 5},
                {7, 9},
                {2, 1},
                {8, 4},
                {5, 6}
        };

        double perimeter = calculatePerimeter(points);
        System.out.println("The perimeter of the polygon is " + perimeter);
    }

    public static double calculatePerimeter(int[][] points) {
        double perimeter = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < points.length; i++) {
            int[] start = points[i];
            int[] end = points[(i + 1) % points.length]; // wrap back to start when we hit the end.

            double distance = Math.sqrt(Math.pow(end[0] - start[0], 2) + Math.pow(end[1] - start[1], 2));
            perimeter += distance;
        }

        return perimeter;
    }
}
```

This Java code assumes that the input array of points is in the order in which they form the polygon. Be careful when you enter these points. If the points are not in order, you won't get the correct result.

The code uses a for loop to calculate the distance between each point and the next one, and adds these distances to a total. When it gets to the end of the array, it wraps back to the start to complete the loop of the polygon (this is what the modulus operator `% points.length` accomplishes). The function then returns the total, which represents the perimeter of the polygon.

