The problem is because writing platform-independent code in C++ to change text color in the console is a complex task. This is due to the significant variation in how different operating systems handle console colors.

While ansi escape sequences are used on Linux and MacOS to manage console output color, Windows uses a different approach that involves calling console specific API functions. This is further complicated by different versions of Windows OS having dissimilar levels of support for ansi escape sequences.

However, here's a minimal version of how it can be done on each system:

On Linux/Mac OS:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
// Escape sequence to set color. Foreground text is 30 to 37, background is 40 to 47
#define SETCOLOR "\033[1m\033[32m" //1m is for bright colors, 32 is for green foreground
#define RESETCOLOR "\033[0m" // Escape sequence to reset color to console default

int main() 
{
    std::cout << SETCOLOR << "Colorful Text!" << RESETCOLOR << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

On Windows, console-specific functions must be used. Here is a example:

```cpp
#include <windows.h> //The Windows API
#include <iostream>

void SetColor(int ForgC)
{
    WORD wColor;
    HANDLE hStdOut = GetStdHandle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);
    CONSOLE_SCREEN_BUFFER_INFO csbi;

    if(GetConsoleScreenBufferInfo(hStdOut, &csbi))
    {
        wColor = (csbi.wAttributes & 0xF0) + (ForgC & 0x0F);
        SetConsoleTextAttribute(hStdOut, wColor);
    }
    return;
}

int main()
{
    SetColor(10); //Green
    std::cout<<"Green text!";
    SetColor(15); //Back to white
    return 0;
}
```

