```Java
import java.util.*;

public class Sorter {

    public static void radixSort(Integer[] arr) {
        if(arr == null){
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input array cannot be null");
        }
        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE, min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (Integer i : arr) {
            if (i != null) {
                max = Math.max(max, i);
                min = Math.min(min, i);
            }
        }

        int offset = 0;
        if (min < 0) {
            offset = -min;
            for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
                arr[i] = arr[i] + offset;
            }
            max = max + offset;
        }

        int maxNumDigits = (int)Math.log10(max) + 1;
        ArrayList<Integer> output[] = new ArrayList[10];
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            output[i] = new ArrayList<>();
        }

        for (int pos = 0; pos < maxNumDigits; pos++) {
            int mask = (int) Math.pow(10, pos);
            for (Integer i : arr) {
                if (i != null) {
                    int digit = (i / mask) % 10;
                    output[digit].add(i);
                }
            }

            int idx = 0;

            for (int i = 9; i >= 0; i--) {
                for (Integer num : output[i]) {
                    arr[idx++] = num;
                }
                output[i].clear();
            }
        }

        if (offset > 0) {
            for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
                arr[i] = arr[i] - offset;
            }
        }

        for(int i=0; i<arr.length/2; i++){ 
            int temp = arr[i]; 
            arr[i] = arr[arr.length -i -1]; 
            arr[arr.length -i -1] = temp; 
        }
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        Integer arr[] = {542, 291, -231, 1617, 898, 23, -9, null, 505, 607, 0, -17, 26, null, 100};
        radixSort(arr);
        for (final int i : arr) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
    }
}
```
This program sorts the array in descending order with radix sort algorithm. The radix sort algorithm proceeds by sorting the input on each digit, from least significant to most significant. For each digit, it distributes the numbers according to their digit values, collects them, and starts again with the next more significant digit.

This implementation also handles null values, and negative numbers. For handling negative numbers, all numbers are shifted upwards by an offset so they can be sorted as they were positive. After sorting, all numbers are shifted back.

Null values are handled by being left out of the radix sorting process entirely, and then they are appended to the end of the sorted array. This is in accordance with common conventions for handling null in sorting. 

Finally, the sorted array is reversed to achieve descending order. The swapping of array elements is done in a for loop that iterates until the midpoint of the array, swapping corresponding elements from the beginning and end of the array.

