Your code in C prints 20 for both because it returns addresses of local variable which is not valid since local variable get destroyed when the function finishes. C compiler didn't prevent you from doing it but in Rust you can't do it because of its ownership rules. 

However we can use `Box` to test this sort of scenario in Rust.

Here's how you can write the code in Rust:

```rust
fn fun() -> Box<i32> {
    let a = 10;
    Box::new(a)
}

fn fun2() -> Box<i32> {
    let b = 20;
    Box::new(b)
}

fn main() {
    let a = fun();
    let b = fun2();
    println!("a:{} b:{}", a, b);
}
```

In this Rust version, `fun` and `fun2` create a heap memory (unlike stack for a and b in your C code) and returns a reference (i.e., Rc<T>) to the heap memory. `Box<i32>` means it is a heap variable of type i32. So I create those variables `a` and `b` with values 10 and 20 respectively on heap and return the Box pointing to those values.

