Sure, one can implement Singleton design pattern in Java using different methods. Here's an implementation using lazy initialization:

```java
public class Singleton {
    // Step 1: Declare a static variable of type Singleton
    private static Singleton singleInstance;

    // Step 2: Private constructor to prevent instantiation from other classes
    private Singleton() {}

    // Step 3: Static method to provide a global point of access
    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        // Step 4: If there isn't already an instance, create one
        if (singleInstance == null) {
            singleInstance = new Singleton();
        }

        // If there is already an instance, return it
        return singleInstance;
    }
}
```
It's important to note that this code guarantees thread safety as the getInstance() method is 'synchronized', meaning only one thread can execute it at a time. However, it introduces unnecessary overhead as the synchronization is not needed once the instance variable is initialized. 

To overcome this issue, one might prefer double-checked locking method like so:

```java
public class Singleton {
  private volatile static Singleton instance;

  private Singleton() {}

  public static Singleton getInstance() {
    if (instance == null) {
      synchronized (Singleton.class) {
        if (instance == null) {
          instance = new Singleton();
        }
      }
    }
    return instance;
  }
}
```
In the double-checked locking method, the synchronized block is used inside the if condition with an additional check to ensure that only one instance of singleton class is created. The 'volatile' keyword ensures that multiple threads handle the uniqueInstance variable correctly when it is being initialized to a Singleton instance.

