Partial methods were introduced in C# 3.0 primarily as a mechanism for auto-generated code to provide hooks where user-code can customize behavior without modifying the auto-generated code itself, while also avoiding any potential performance overhead if the user-define hooks are not used. They are predominantly used by code generation tools, like Visual Studio's designer tools, or ORM tools like Entity Framework.

For example, consider Entity Framework. EF will auto-generate classes based on your database schema, typically with a `.designer.cs` file extension. Partial methods can be declared in these classes corresponding to each property, indicating before/after each change. As a developer, you can then write an implementation for these in a separate file, and the compiler will link them up. This way, you can add custom code that runs when a property changes, without having to either modify auto-generated code, or worry about the performance implications of event handlers that are hooked up but do nothing. 

Suppose you have an auto-generated class like this:

```C#
public partial class Customer {
    partial void OnFirstNameChanging(string value);
    private string _firstName;
    public string FirstName {
        get { return _firstName; }
        set {
            OnFirstNameChanging(value);
            _firstName = value;
        }
    }
}
```
In this case, when the `FirstName` is updated, the method `OnFirstNameChanging` is called.

As a developer, you can then implement this method in a separate file, in the same project:

```C#
public partial class Customer {
    partial void OnFirstNameChanging(string value) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(value)) {
            throw new ArgumentException("First name cannot be empty");
        }
    }
}
```

Now, whenever the `FirstName` property changes, the `OnFirstNameChanging` method will execute this validation code and potentially throw an exception.

This separation preserves encapsulation, modularity, and cohesion principles while ensuring the adaptability and robustness of your code. But remember, partial methods cannot have access modifiers, cannot have return values, cannot have out parameters, and cannot be virtual/override/abstract/sealed/new. 

So it is recommended to use partial methods with caution, and primarily in code-generation situations where it's necessary to modify the behavior of auto-generated code without directly editing it.

