In the C-programming language, you can determine the harmonic mean with the following code snippet.

```C
#include <stdio.h>

double harmonicMean(int a[], int n) 
{
    double sum = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) 
    {
        sum = sum + (1 / (double)a[i]);
    }

    return (double)n/sum;
}

int main() 
{
    int values[] = {7, 8, 9, 10, 11};
    int n = sizeof(values)/sizeof(values[0]);

    printf("Harmonic Mean of the array is %.6lf", harmonicMean(values, n));

    return 0;
}
```

This code first declares a `harmonicMean` function that takes in an integer array and its length. It then computes the sum of reciprocals of all given numbers. Following this, it evaluates the harmonic mean by dividing the count of numbers by the summation of all reciprocals obtained.

The `main()` function initialize an array `values[]` and calculates its size. Using the `printf` statement, it calls the `harmonicMean` function and prints the output on the screen. The `%.6lf` in `printf` is used to print the output up to 6 decimal places.

Note: This code assumes that none of the elements in the array is 0 as division by 0 (while calculating harmonic mean) may lead to an exception or undefined behavior.

