There isn't a built-in automated process in Visual Studio for hiding reports or adding users in SSRS after deployment. Generally, these sort of administration tasks are performed in the Report Manager or SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS) after deployment.

However, you can automate these tasks using the Report Server Web Service, if you're comfortable with writing some code. The Report Server Web Service provides methods to apply security settings and hide reports.

Here is an example code snippet to hide a report using the Report Server Web Service:

```csharp
ReportingService2010 rs = new ReportingService2010();
rs.Credentials = System.Net.CredentialCache.DefaultCredentials;

CatalogItem item = new CatalogItem();
item.Hidden = true;

rs.SetProperties("/path/to/your/report", new Property[] { item });
```

And here is an example for setting security permissions on a report:

```csharp
ReportingService2010 rs = new ReportingService2010();
rs.Credentials = System.Net.CredentialCache.DefaultCredentials;

Policy newPolicy = new Policy();
newPolicy.GroupUserName = @"domain\user";
newPolicy.Roles = new Role[] { new Role() { Name = "Browser" } }; // assign the "Browser" role

rs.SetPolicies("/path/to/your/report", new Policy[] { newPolicy });
```

