Correct and optimize the given Java code to generate a Fibonacci sequence using dynamic programming:

The code below attempts to implement a Fibonacci sequence generator. It attempts to implement recursion but is erroneous and inefficient. Your task is to debug the code and further optimize it using dynamic programming, thereby reducing its time complexity.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {   
    public int fib(int n) {
        if (n === 0) return 0;
        if (n === 1) return 1;
        return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2); 
    }
}
```

The existing Java Fibonacci solution uses recursion that leads to overlapping subproblems resulting in significant performance reduction. It has a time complexity of O(2^n).

Here's the optimized version of above code using Dynamic Programming - Memoization to store intermediate results which will be used to compute subsequent solutions without repeating the work.

Using dynamic programming, the time complexity will be improved to linear O(n).

Here's the corrected and optimized code for Fibonacci sequence:

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] fibCache;

    public int fib(int n) {
        if (n < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("n cannot be less than zero");
        }

        fibCache = new int[n + 1];
        Arrays.fill(fibCache, -1);  // Fill the array with -1 for unused values

        return fibonacci(n);
    }

    private int fibonacci(int n) {
        if (n == 0) return 0;
        if (n == 1) return 1;

        // if fib(n) already computed, return from cache array
        if (fibCache[n] != -1) return fibCache[n];

        // if fib(n) not already computed, compute and store in cache array
        fibCache[n] = fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
        return fibCache[n];
    }
}

```
The class now features a private method `fibonacci`, which is used for recursion and stores the results of each calculated Fibonacci number. The `fib` function now includes input checking, allows for reinitializing the `fibCache`, and fills it with -1 values (an indication these are yet to be computed).

