You can implement a classic stack in Java using nodes. Here are a few steps you can follow to do this:

1. Define a Node class with two properties: value and nextNode. 

2. Define a Stack class with a property topNode which stores the reference to the top node of the stack.

3. In the Stack class, define the following methods:

    a. `push(element)`: Add an element to the stack. Create a new node with the `element` value and make its `nextNode` the current `topNode` of the stack. Update the `topNode` of the stack to this new node.

    b. `pop()`: Remove the top element of the stack. If the stack is not empty, set the `topNode` to its `nextNode`. Return the value of the node that was removed.

    c. `peek()`: Return the value of `topNode` without removing it from the stack. If the stack is empty, return `null`. 

    d. `isEmpty()`: Return a boolean value indicating whether the stack is empty (i.e., the `topNode` is `null`).

Here's some skeleton code that should help guide your implementation:

```java
class Node {
    int value;
    Node nextNode;

    public Node(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        nextNode = null;
    }
}

class Stack {
    Node topNode;

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (topNode == null);
    }

    public void push(int value) {
        Node newNode = new Node(value);
        if (!isEmpty()) {
            newNode.nextNode = topNode;
        }
        topNode = newNode;
    }

    public Integer pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) return null;

        int poppedValue = topNode.value;
        topNode = topNode.nextNode;
        return poppedValue;
    }

    public Integer peek() {
        if (isEmpty()) return null;

        return topNode.value;
    }
}
```

