Here's a simple implementation of Bubble Sort and Quick Sort. It uses a User Interface to let users choose which sorting algorithm to use. 

It's recommended to write a class for each sorting algorithm but for simplicity, they are implemented as static methods in the same class.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class SortingAlgorithms {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create an array of 10 random integers
        int[] matrix = new Random().ints(10, 1, 100).toArray();
        System.out.println("Initial array: " + Arrays.toString(matrix));

        System.out.println("Choose sorting algorithm:\n 1. Bubble Sort \n 2. Quick Sort");
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int choice = scanner.nextInt();

        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                bubbleSort(matrix);
                System.out.println("Array after Bubble Sort: " + Arrays.toString(matrix));
                break;
            case 2:
                quickSort(matrix, 0, matrix.length - 1);
                System.out.println("Array after Quick Sort: " + Arrays.toString(matrix));
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid Choice");
        }

        scanner.close();
    }

    public static void bubbleSort(int[] matrix) {
        int n = matrix.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++)
                if (matrix[j] > matrix[j+1]) {
                    // swap matrix[j+1] and matrix[j]
                    int temp = matrix[j];
                    matrix[j] = matrix[j+1];
                    matrix[j+1] = temp;
                }
    }

    public static void quickSort(int[] matrix, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            // find pivot element such that elements smaller than pivot are on the left 
            // and elements greater than pivot are on the right
            int pi = partition(matrix, low, high);

            // Recursively sort elements on the left of pivot and on the right of pivot
            quickSort(matrix, low, pi-1);
            quickSort(matrix, pi+1, high);
        }
    }

    static int partition(int[] matrix, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = matrix[high]; 
        int i = (low-1); 
        for (int j=low; j<high; j++) {
            if (matrix[j] < pivot) {
                i++;

                // swap matrix[i] and matrix[j]
                int temp = matrix[i];
                matrix[i] = matrix[j];
                matrix[j] = temp;
            }
        }

        // swap matrix[i+1] and matrix[high] (or pivot)
        int temp = matrix[i+1];
        matrix[i+1] = matrix[high];
        matrix[high] = temp;

        return i+1;
    }
}
```

This program first creates an array of 10 random integers between 1 and 100. It then asks the user to select a sorting algorithm: 1 for Bubble Sort and 2 for Quick Sort. If the user selects an invalid option, the program prints "Invalid Choice". After choosing the sorting algorithm, the program sorts the array and prints the sorted array.

Please note that this is a simple implementation and has room for improvement, such as handling exceptions if the user inputs a non-integer or an integer that is not 1 or 2.

