An isogram is a word or phrase without a repeating letter, where ignoring capitalization is allowed.

Here is a simple JavaScript program that accomplishes this:

```javascript
function isIsogram(sentence) {
  // Convert the text to lower case and remove any white space
  sentence = sentence.toLowerCase().replace(/[\s]/g, '');

  // Split the sentence into an array of characters
  let characters = sentence.split('');

  // Use a set to check for duplicated characters
  let charSet = new Set(characters);

  // If the length of the array and the size of the set are equal
  // then every character in the sentence is unique, so it's an isogram
  return charSet.size === characters.length;
}

// Test the function
console.log(isIsogram("Turbo fan")); // Outputs: true
console.log(isIsogram("I am not an isogram")); // Outputs: false
```

This program works by first converting the string to lower case and removing all the white spaces. After that, it splits the string into an array of characters and adds all the characters to a set.

A set in JavaScript is a collection of unique values, so if a character is duplicated in the original sentence, it will only appear once in the set. Therefore, if the sentence is an isogram, the size of the set (i.e., the number of unique characters) will be equal to the length of the array (i.e., the total number of characters). If the sentence is not an isogram, the size of the set will be less than the length of the array.

