Here, the implementation of the `circularShift` function uses the `reverse` function of vector in which we firstly reverse the whole array then reverse the first k elements and then the remaining elements after the k elements.

In terms of time complexity, the writing operation of reverse function works in linear time. Thus the overall time complexity of this program is `O(n)`. In terms of space complexity, as the `reverse` operation swaps the elements in-place in the array, it has a space complexity of `O(1)`.

The `circularShift` function also handles edge cases such as an empty array and negative shift values by checking for these conditions before performing the shift operation. If the shift number is equal to zero, the array will remain the same, so no shift operation is performed. If the shift number is greater than the size of the array, the shift number is reduced to its modulus with respect to the size of the array to bring the shift number within the size of the array.

The `printArray` function is used to print the array after each shift operation.

