The reason why the procedural code does not get the same result as the object-oriented code is because the `date_timestamp_get()` function does not account for time zones, while initializing the DateTime object with DateTimeZone accounts for the timezone.

The PHP `date_timestamp_get()` function does not take the time zone into account when giving you the timestamp, it merely gives you the timestamp of the date/time object without any offsets.

In contrast, when you initialize your DateTime object with the DateTimeZone, it understands you want the current datetime including the offset for the `America/Los_Angeles` timezone. So when you get a formatted date/time, it returns the correct value since it's considering the offset value of the timezone from UTC.

The `date_timestamp_get()` can therefore give you different results depending on your server timezone settings or if you have used `date_default_timezone_set()`. In most cases, this will likely be set to UTC.

