Here is a basic implementation of the task in C++. The program starts a separate thread to print each character and ensures they print in the correct order. It catches invalid arguments at runtime and ensures each string is not null.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>

class StringPrinter {
    std::string str;
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    std::mutex mtx;
    int current = 0;

public:
    StringPrinter(const std::string &s) : str(s) {}

    void printChar(int index, char c) {
        while (true) {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            if (current == index) {
                std::cout << c;
                ++current;
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    void print() {
        for (int i = 0; i < str.size(); ++i) {
            threads.push_back(std::thread(&StringPrinter::printChar, this, i, str[i]));
        }

        for (auto &th : threads) {
            th.join();
        }
    }
};

int main(int argc, char* argv[])
{
    if (argc != 2) {
        std::cerr << "Usage: " << argv[0] << " <string>" << std::endl;
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    try {
        StringPrinter printer(argv[1]);
        printer.print();
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }
    catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << e.what() << std::endl;
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

This program creates an object from the StringPrinter class using the input string. Then, it starts a separate thread for each character in the string and waits for all of them to finish using join(). In the StringPrinter class, the printChar() function does the actual printing. Each thread calls this function with a unique index and the character to print. If the index matches the current index to print, it prints the character and increments the index. Otherwise, it keeps looping until it's its turn. A mutex ensures that only one thread can change the current index and print a character at the same time. This ensures that the output order is correct and prevents data races.

Please note that the order of thread execution might not correspond to the order of thread creation, even though each character is printed in the right order. Thus, this program might create unnecessary threads that keep looping until it's their turn. However, it demonstrates the concept of synchronization and data race prevention. There are much more efficient ways to solve this problem without creating a separate thread for each character.

