In below Java method, we are using a HashSet to collect unique words in given sentence and then applying the logic to append a specific string at the initial part of each unique word.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(addPrefixToUniqueWords("The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog", "PRE_"));
    }

    public static String addPrefixToUniqueWords(String sentence, String prefix) {
        // Handling case when sentence or/and prefix is null
        if(sentence == null || prefix == null)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input sentence and prefix should not be null.");

        // Splitting the sentence into words
        String[] words = sentence.split("\\s+");

        // A set to hold unique words in sentence
        Set<String> uniqueWords = new HashSet<>();

        // Loop to collect unique words
        for(String word : words){
            uniqueWords.add(word);
        }

        // Loop to modify unique words
        for(String uniqueWord : uniqueWords){
            sentence = sentence.replaceFirst(uniqueWord, prefix + uniqueWord);
        }

        // Return sentence with prefix added to unique words
        return sentence;
    }
}
```
In above code, unique words will have "PRE_" at the start assuming "PRE_" was the prefix passed. Note, replaceFirst method treats input as regex and if your uniqueWord has regex special characters (like \. $ ^ etc.), it will create issue. So for real world usage of this code, you have to escape those special regex characters.

